        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Verdict Up Front
          </h2>

          <p>
            For homeowners, renters, hobbyists and anyone who uses power tools a few times a month, <strong>Ryobi ONE+ is the best value in cordless tools</strong>. The range is enormous, the batteries are shared across everything from drills to fans to glue guns, and the HP brushless line delivers enough power for decks, fences, furniture builds and most remodeling projects.
          </p>

          <p>
            For professionals who run tools all day, Ryobi is the wrong main platform. Gearboxes, chucks, switches and housings are built to a price, and owners who push them on jobsites commonly report wear sooner than with professional brands. The three-year standard tool warranty is shorter than Milwaukee's five, and service options are thinner.
          </p>

          <p>
            The sensible approach is to buy the right Ryobi tools and skip the wrong ones. HP brushless drills, impact drivers, oscillating tools, trimmers, inflators, work lights and specialty tools are excellent values. Standard brushed versions of high-load tools such as circular saws and reciprocating saws are where Ryobi feels most like a budget brand.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are weighing Ryobi against the professional brands, our <a href="/milwaukee-vs-dewalt/">Milwaukee vs DeWalt comparison</a> explains what the extra money buys, so you can judge whether you need it.
          </p>

