        <div id="platform" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The ONE+ Platform
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ryobi launched ONE+ in 1996 with a simple promise: any ONE+ battery fits any ONE+ tool, past or future. Ryobi has kept that promise through several battery chemistries and form factors, which is unusual in an industry that often changes battery designs. A lithium pack bought today will run a ONE+ tool from many years ago, and the reverse is broadly true too, though older nickel-cadmium packs deliver far less performance.
          </p>

          <p>
            The lineup is the widest in the industry, with hundreds of tools. Beyond the expected drills, saws and sanders, ONE+ includes hot glue guns, caulk guns, inflators, fans, work lights, Bluetooth speakers, hand vacuums, power scrubbers, soldering irons, a cordless nailer that needs no compressor, and even a cooler and a bug zapper. If you can imagine a battery tool for the house, Ryobi probably makes it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ryobi's power tool brand is licensed to Techtronic Industries, the same company that owns Milwaukee. The two are engineered and marketed separately, and their batteries are not compatible. Ryobi's job inside TTI is the consumer market, which is why its tools are priced and built for weekend users rather than trades.
          </p>

          <p>
            In the United States Ryobi power tools are sold primarily through Home Depot, along with Ryobi's own site. That makes prices consistent and returns easy, but it also means you will not find Ryobi at Lowe's or most independent hardware stores. Reconditioned Ryobi tools are sold through factory outlet channels at a discount, often with a shorter warranty.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">WHY IT MATTERS</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Compatibility is Ryobi's best feature.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A battery platform that does not change shape every few years protects your investment. The batteries you buy for a drill will run a trimmer, a fan and an inflator for years to come.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="hp-vs-standard" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. HP Brushless vs Standard Tools
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ryobi sells most core tools in two tiers. <strong>Standard ONE+</strong> tools are typically brushed, lighter on features and cheapest. <strong>ONE+ HP</strong> tools use brushless motors with more advanced electronics, and Ryobi says they are designed to draw more power from its high-performance batteries. Most HP tools are noticeably more compact and more powerful than their standard counterparts.
          </p>

          <p>
            Brushless motors convert more battery energy into work and less into heat, so they run longer per charge and hold power under load. In practice the difference shows up most in drills driving large bits or long screws, impact drivers sinking lag bolts, and saws cutting thick or wet lumber. Owners commonly report that the HP drill and impact feel like a different class of tool from the older brushed versions.
          </p>

          <p>
            For light tasks, standard tools are fine. A brushed drill handles pilot holes, furniture assembly and hanging shelves without complaint, and a brushed oscillating tool or sander does its job. The extra cost of HP pays off on tools that work hard: drills, impact drivers, circular saws, reciprocating saws, grinders and string trimmers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pairing matters too. HP tools reach their best performance on Ryobi's higher-output packs. Running an HP circular saw on a small 1.5 or 2.0 amp-hour pack will work but limits both runtime and power under load. Budget for at least one larger pack if you buy high-draw HP tools.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Platform Facts</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1996</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Year ONE+ launched with a compatibility pledge</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">18V</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Nominal voltage of ONE+ batteries</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">3 years</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Standard limited warranty on ONE+ tools</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Hundreds</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Tools that share the ONE+ battery</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="best-tools" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Ryobi Tools Worth Buying
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>HP brushless drill and impact driver.</strong> These are the core of any kit, and Ryobi's HP versions are strong enough for decks, fences, framing small projects, and assembling furniture all weekend. They are lighter and more compact than older Ryobi drills and usually sold in kits with two batteries at a price that undercuts professional brands by a wide margin.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Specialty household tools.</strong> This is where Ryobi has no real competition. The cordless inflator fills car tires, bike tires and sports balls. The hot glue gun, caulk gun and power scrubber save time on chores most people do by hand. Fans, work lights and the battery-powered speaker are inexpensive add-ons that make the platform more useful every week, not just on project days.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Oscillating multi-tool and random orbit sander.</strong> Both are light-duty by nature, and Ryobi's versions do the job well for trim cuts, flush cuts on door casings, sanding furniture and smoothing patches. Professional versions offer better vibration control and blade change systems, but most homeowners will not feel the difference on occasional jobs.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Yard tools on ONE+.</strong> The 18V HP string trimmers, hedge trimmers and blowers are good for small and medium lots. For larger properties, Ryobi's 40V line is stronger. Many owners find the ONE+ trimmer and blower an easy way to retire a gas tool on a quarter-acre lot.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Cordless nailers.</strong> Ryobi's AirStrike nailers drive brad and finish nails without a compressor or gas cartridge. They are slower to fire than pneumatic nailers but convenient for trim, baseboard and small projects. Owners commonly report that they are one of the platform's best surprises.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Tools to Skip or Upgrade
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Standard brushed circular saws.</strong> They cut plywood and two-by lumber, but they slow down in thick or wet stock and drain batteries quickly. If you plan to cut more than occasional boards, buy the HP brushless version or a corded saw. A circular saw is where a weak motor turns into a frustrating, sometimes unsafe, binding cut.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Low-end reciprocating saws.</strong> Ryobi's basic brushed reciprocating saw is fine for pruning and occasional demolition, but it vibrates more and lacks the power for heavy demolition or cutting through nails embedded in lumber. Step up to the HP model, or a professional brand if you demolish regularly.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Tools where precision matters.</strong> Ryobi's table and miter saws work for rough carpentry, but finish carpenters often find fences, detents and blade alignment less precise than higher-end saws. For fine woodworking or trim work where small errors show, a dedicated saw from a woodworking-focused brand is worth the money. The same thinking applies to a <a href="/best-benchtop-planer/">benchtop planer</a>, where precision matters more than battery convenience.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Measuring tools.</strong> Ryobi makes laser levels and stud finders, but specialist brands often do these better. For hanging a television or tiling a room, a well-reviewed tool from our <a href="/best-laser-level/">best laser levels</a> and <a href="/best-stud-finder/">best stud finders</a> guides is a safer bet than whatever is cheapest on the battery platform you own.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Hand tools in place of a real set.</strong> Ryobi's power tools are the draw; for sockets, wrenches and pliers, a budget specialist such as Tekton offers better quality per dollar. See our <a href="/tekton-tools-review/">Tekton tools review</a> for details.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="batteries" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Batteries and Chargers
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ryobi sells ONE+ lithium packs from compact 1.5 and 2.0 amp-hour sizes up to larger 4.0, 6.0 and higher capacity packs, plus HP high-performance packs designed for brushless tools. Compact packs keep a drill light; large packs are for saws, trimmers and blowers. Most owners settle on two compact and one or two large packs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lithium-ion batteries wear with charge cycles, heat and time. A ONE+ pack used regularly typically gives several years of service before capacity drops noticeably. Store packs indoors, away from freezing garages and hot cars, and avoid leaving them fully discharged for long periods. A pack stored at partial charge in a cool room ages more slowly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Chargers matter more than people think. Basic chargers are slow, sometimes several hours for a large pack. Ryobi's rapid chargers and multi-port chargers cut that time substantially and are worth buying if you own large packs. Charging a hot battery immediately after heavy use can be delayed by the charger's thermal protection; that is normal.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ryobi frequently bundles batteries with free tools, especially around holidays and spring. Watch for battery kits that include a bare tool of your choice. Buying batteries this way is often cheaper than buying the same battery alone and the tool separately. Avoid unofficial third-party batteries; poor cells and missing protection circuits are a real fire risk.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="outdoor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. The 40V Outdoor Line
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ryobi runs a separate <strong>40V</strong> platform for larger outdoor equipment such as mowers, chainsaws, blowers, string trimmers and snow blowers, plus an 80V platform for riding mowers. The 40V batteries do not fit ONE+ tools, so this is a second platform, but it is designed for homeowners with larger lots who need more power and runtime than 18V tools deliver.
          </p>

          <p>
            The 40V HP brushless mowers and blowers have become serious alternatives to gas for suburban yards. They start instantly, run quietly and need almost no maintenance. Runtime depends on the battery size and how thick the grass is; owners commonly add a second battery to finish larger lawns without stopping.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ryobi's 40V tools generally carry a longer warranty than ONE+ tools, often five years on the tool, with separate terms for batteries. Check current terms when you buy. For larger properties or heavier use, EGO's 56V platform is the main competitor and is often stronger, especially in mowers and blowers. For chainsaws specifically, see our <a href="/best-chainsaw/">best chainsaw guide</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you only have a small yard, stay on ONE+ for outdoor tools and avoid buying a second battery platform. An 18V HP trimmer, blower and hedge trimmer handle a quarter-acre lot comfortably. Move to 40V only if you need a battery mower or heavy outdoor tools.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Warranty, Service and Where to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most ONE+ tools carry a three-year limited warranty, and ONE+ batteries generally carry a three-year limited warranty as well, with terms varying by product. Reconditioned tools sold through Ryobi's outlet channels usually carry shorter coverage. Keep receipts and register tools; claims are much simpler with proof of purchase.
          </p>

          <p>
            Warranty service is handled through authorized service centers and Ryobi's customer service line. In practice, many owners report that a defective tool within the first 90 days is easiest to resolve with an exchange at Home Depot. After that, the warranty process can take longer. For a tool that costs relatively little, some owners simply replace it rather than ship it for repair.
          </p>

          <p>
            Home Depot is the main retailer in the United States, both in store and online. Prices are consistent, and promotions are frequent: holiday, Father's Day and spring events often include free tools with battery kits or deep discounts on combo kits. Patient buyers rarely need to pay full price.
          </p>

          <p>
            Reconditioned tools can be a great deal for light users. They are factory-inspected returns or overstock and often sell for a significant discount. Check the warranty terms before buying, since they differ from new tools.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-pro" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Ryobi vs Professional Brands
          </h2>

          <p>
            Compared with Milwaukee and DeWalt, Ryobi tools are usually less durable under daily use, a bit heavier for the same power in some categories, and simpler in features. They are also much cheaper. A Ryobi HP drill and impact kit often costs a fraction of a premium professional kit, and the gap grows as you add tools.
          </p>

          <p>
            For homeowners, that trade-off usually favors Ryobi. A drill used a few times a month for a decade will not wear out the way it would on a job site, and the breadth of household tools on ONE+ adds real convenience. For trades, the trade-off favors professional brands, since downtime and replacement costs outweigh the initial savings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ryobi also competes with Craftsman, Black and Decker, Hart and Ridgid. Ridgid, also sold at Home Depot and made by TTI, sits between Ryobi and Milwaukee in price and build and offers a lifetime service agreement on registered tools, which is worth a look for serious hobbyists. Craftsman and Hart are closer to Ryobi in price; neither has Ryobi's breadth.
          </p>

          <p>
            The best approach for many households is a mix: Ryobi for the broad set of everyday power tools, a good hand-tool set for mechanical work, and specialist tools where precision matters, such as a calibrated wrench from our <a href="/best-torque-wrench/">best torque wrench guide</a> for wheels and brakes.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Buy HP for tools that work hard, standard for tools that do not.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Spend on brushless HP versions of drills, impacts and saws. Save on lights, fans, inflators, glue guns and other light-duty tools, where standard models do the job well.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying brushed saws for real work</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Standard brushed circular and reciprocating saws work for occasional cuts, but they bog down in thick or wet lumber and drain batteries fast. If you will cut regularly, the HP brushless version costs a little more and performs much better. A weak saw also binds more, which raises the risk of kickback.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running high-draw tools on tiny packs</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A compact 1.5 or 2.0 amp-hour battery keeps a drill light, but on a circular saw, blower or trimmer it limits power and runtime. Buy at least one larger or high-performance pack for demanding tools. Ryobi's HP tools are designed to deliver their best performance on those packs.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Starting a second battery platform without need</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ryobi's 40V outdoor line is excellent, but it uses different batteries from ONE+. If your yard is small, ONE+ HP trimmers and blowers will do the job, and you avoid buying a second set of batteries and chargers. Move to 40V only for a mower or heavier tools.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying full price</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ryobi runs frequent promotions, especially at Home Depot during spring, Father's Day and holiday sales. Battery kits with a free bare tool and discounted combo kits are common. Buying outside a promotion often means paying noticeably more than needed for the same tools and batteries.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using off-brand batteries</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cheap third-party ONE+ compatible batteries are widely sold online. Many lack the protection circuits and quality cells of genuine packs, which can mean poor performance and a real fire risk. They also void coverage if they damage a tool. Buy genuine Ryobi batteries, ideally during a promotion.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a new Ryobi battery fit my old ONE+ tools?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Ryobi designed ONE+ so that any ONE+ battery fits any ONE+ tool, and it has kept that compatibility since the platform launched in 1996. A current lithium pack runs older tools, and older lithium packs run new tools. Nickel-cadmium packs from early years also fit, but deliver far less runtime and power than current lithium packs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are Ryobi tools good enough for remodeling a house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a homeowner doing their own remodeling, yes, especially the HP brushless drills, impact drivers and saws. They handle framing small walls, installing cabinets, building decks and similar projects. For contractors running tools daily, professional brands last longer and offer better service. Many DIY remodelers start with Ryobi and add specialist tools as needed.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Ryobi HP worth the extra money?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For drills, impact drivers, circular saws, reciprocating saws and string trimmers, usually yes. The brushless motors deliver more power, run longer per charge and are more compact. For light-duty tools like lights, fans, glue guns and inflators, the standard versions are fine. Spend where the tool works hard and save where it does not.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do Ryobi tools typically last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                With normal household use, many owners report Ryobi drills and impacts lasting many years. Batteries typically give several years of service before capacity drops noticeably. Durability falls off under daily professional use, where gearboxes, chucks and switches wear faster. Storing tools and batteries indoors and avoiding heat extends their life considerably.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I buy Ryobi tools anywhere besides Home Depot?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In the United States, Home Depot is the main retailer, along with Ryobi's own website. Reconditioned Ryobi tools are sold through factory outlet channels. Some tools appear through other marketplaces, but buying from authorized sellers protects your warranty and helps you avoid counterfeit batteries, which are a known problem.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What should my first Ryobi purchase be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A ONE+ HP brushless drill and impact driver kit with two batteries and a charger is the best start for most people. It covers drilling and driving for nearly every household project. Add a circular saw, an oscillating tool and an inflator next, buying bare tools during promotions to build the kit without paying for extra chargers.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How does Ryobi compare with Ridgid?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Ridgid is also made by TTI and sold at Home Depot. It sits above Ryobi in price and build quality, and registered Ridgid tools can qualify for a lifetime service agreement. Ryobi has the broader lineup and lower prices. For homeowners Ryobi is usually the better value; for serious hobbyists who want more durability, Ridgid is worth comparing.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

